      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Two common clinical syndromes of acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) hypersensitivity, aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) and aspirin-exacerbated cutaneous disease (AECD), were subjected to a genome-wide association study to identify strong genetic markers for aspirin hypersensitivity in a Korean population."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A comparison of SNP genotype frequencies on an Affymetrix Genome-Wide Human SNP array of 179 AERD patients and 1989 healthy normal control subjects (NC) revealed SNPs on chromosome 6 that were associated with AERD, but not AECD. To validate the association, we enrolled a second cohort comprising AERD (n = 264), NC (n = 238) and disease-control (aspirin tolerant asthma; ATA, n = 387) groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The minor genotype frequency (AG or AA) of a particular SNP, rs3128965, in the HLA-DPB1 region was higher in the AERD group compared to the ATA or NC group (P = 0.001, P = 0.002, in a co-dominant analysis model, respectively). Comparison of rs3128965 alleles with the clinical features of asthmatics revealed that patients harboring the A allele had increased bronchial hyperresponsiveness to inhaled aspirin and methacholine, and higher 15-HETE levels, than those without the A allele (P = 0.039, 0.037, and 0.004, respectively)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This implies the potential of rs3128965 as a genetic marker for diagnosis and prediction of the AERD phenotype."
        }
      ]},
